  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 100 PM CDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nMidday infrared satellite imagery continued to highlight a\nperfectly clear sky with surface observations outlining light\nwinds. It has been another beautiful midsummer morning, but\nafternoon temperatures will increase noticeably, well into the\n90s, as midlevel high pressure begins to encroach from the west.\nNot as cool tonight, with light south winds and a warmer\natmosphere holding all locations in the upper 60s. \n\nMonday is expected to be the hottest day of the upcoming week. \nA plume of very hot air at 850 mb over the Great Basin and\ncentral Rockies Sunday will arrive in SW KS Monday, with 850 mb\ntemperatures as high as 35C. All models show this strong warming\nMonday, and even the NBM has correctly trending hotter. Added\nanother 1-2 degrees to the latest NBM guidance, with afternoon\nair temperatures of 100-105 common. Some record highs are\nexpected to be threatened, particularly Garden City, which has a\nrecord high of 104 and a current gridded forecast of 103.\nFortunately, dewpoints are expected to be confined to the 50s\nall zones Monday - even the eastern zones - such that a heat\nadvisory (heat index of 105 or greater) is not expected to be\nrequired. Monday's heat will be more of the drier, hair \ndryer/blow torch variety, with a significant increase in south \nwinds. Lee cyclogenesis to near 992 mb in eastern Colorado will \nsustain a typical Kansas windy day, with south winds averaging \n20-30 mph and gusts to near 40 mph. NBM probability of\ntemperatures > 100 degrees, west of US 83, is near 100% Monday\nafternoon. \n\nA weak cold front entering northwest Kansas Tuesday morning,\nwill make at least some progress southward into parts of SW KS\nTuesday. Models disagree on the cold front's progression, with\n12z NAM notably the most aggressive. Global models are much\nslower as the boundary encounters hot air in the afternoon.\nRegardless, NBM is a good compromise with variable wind\ndirections and a temperature range across the CWA, ranging from\nnear 90 north to near 103 Barber county. \n\nRain chances through Tuesday are very limited, but not zero. A\nshortwave rounding the northeast periphery of the ridge will\nencourage a few thunderstorms to approach the northwest zones\nMonday evening. 12z NAM/GFS both suggest convective development\nalong the cold/stationary frontal boundary favoring the\nsouthern zones adjacent to Oklahoma Tuesday evening. Along with\nWFO Norman, added some pops to the NBM forecast for this. \nRain chances look more encouraging, compared to previous\nforecasts, for Wednesday and Thursday. With the core of the\nstrong ridge remaining well west of SW KS over the Great Basin,\nthe resulting NW flow aloft will encourage scattered\nthunderstorm activity Wednesday and Thursday nights. NBM pops in\nthe scattered category were accepted. The ridge remaining west\nwill also allow the heat to remain in check, with afternoon\ntemperatures in the 90s, instead of the 100s. \n\nExpect the heat to rebuild and any rain chances end next \nweekend, as the summer ridge axis returns to the plains.",
